module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.node_type_shape_prints_applied_head_without_declaration_identity

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { CeilingNotStated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

// THE §5 DIAGNOSTIC DEFECT THE INFER LANE MEASURED BUT COULD NOT FIX HONESTLY (#14152 PR-2 work;
// stern-bat-401 ruling, option (A)). Filed so the class is on the ledger and, crucially, so the
// FIX that is being held is held in the open -- with its reason and its retirement route recorded --
// rather than shipped blind or dropped silently.
data node_type_shape_prints_applied_head_without_declaration_identity: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "node_type_shape_prints_applied_head_without_declaration_identity" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: CeilingNotStated },
  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: v1.compiler.infer_types node_type_shape renders an APPLIED type by its head NAME and its argument list, and drops the field that makes two applications of the same head distinct -- the declaration the head resolves to. Its applied-head arm prints `Node(<head-name><args>)` off node.name; the Node's declaration identity is available on the node (the type already carries it, and the module already has readers named node_declaration_identity / node_declaration_file / whole_declaration_carried), but the applied-head arm never reads it. The population this projection cannot separate is TWO APPLICATIONS OF DISTINCT DECLARATIONS THAT SHARE ONE RENDERED NAME, with otherwise-equal rendered structure: the name comes from `authored_name_at`, which supplies no declaring identity, so both print the same `Node(<name><args>)`. ATTAINABLE CEILING (DESIGN section 5, fail-closed; item 3 of the side-chat review on head c11e43d, which required an attainable ceiling with its reason rather than a bare below-ladder assertion): the ceiling this row holds itself to is a RENDERED-IDENTITY ceiling -- for any two applied types whose only distinguishing field is the head's resolved declaration, node_type_shape's output must itself distinguish them, so a reader of a diagnostic cannot be handed one string for two distinct types. That ceiling is attainable, and the reason it is available is source-local: the field the arm drops is ALREADY CARRIED on the Node (`node.declaration`), so reaching the ceiling needs no new fact from anywhere -- only that the applied-head arm read the field it already has. DESIGN section 4b, meta-obligation 1 (rung honesty): this is stated as the row's CEILING (what the check must guarantee), NOT as its rung; the reported RUNG stays at the ledger/observation grain the instrument carrier below establishes, and the executed-witness guarantee (the RED/control once the carrier lands) is kept SEPARATE from complete class closure.",
    "HARM: a diagnostic whose ONLY distinguishing identity between two applied types is the head's resolved declaration becomes, under this projection, uninformative -- the expected and got strings collapse to one, so a reader (or a test asserting on the string) sees agreement where there is a real disagreement. SCOPE (side-chat review on head c11e43d, item 3): node_type_shape already distinguishes most names, arguments, and connectives; the harm is confined to the diagnostics whose distinguishing identity THIS projection loses -- the applied-head arm, where two applications of one rendered name to DISTINCT head declarations print byte-for-byte the same string. It is not 'every diagnostic'. GRAIN (item 3, retained): this is an UNINFORMATIVE-DIAGNOSTIC defect at the RENDERED-IDENTITY grain, kept distinct from the checker-refusal class -- even when the checker correctly locates and refuses a wrong program, the expected/got text it emits can collapse; the collapse does not by itself establish that a wrong program was ACCEPTED. SPECIMEN STATUS (item 1): the `Wrapper<KindB>` vs `Wrapper<KindA>` pair named in the module's own comment is HISTORICAL, not current evidence -- `node_type_shape_argument_list` now renders arguments, so those two already print differently, and their shared `Wrapper` head is a SINGLE declaration, so head declaration identity is not what separates that pair. The real omitted population is DISTINCT declarations sharing one rendered name with otherwise-equal rendered structure; no live corpus instance is asserted here, and direct observation of such a rendered pair awaits the named carrier below.",
    "WHY THIS ROW, AND WHY THE CODE IS NOT IN THIS PR (rung stated honestly; DESIGN section 4b meta-obligation 1 -- the reported rung equals the rung established by EXECUTED evidence; a fix with no discriminating input is a §4b decoration and a §5 ship-blind). The fix (give the applied-head arm its declaration identity, alongside the head name it already prints) was MEASURED to have no authorable in-lane discriminator, reported to the manager, and the manager ruled (A): file the row, hold the code. The measurement, in full: (1) a dag/-rooted claim cannot import v1.compiler.* -- CI floor resolution scans the claim lane under the dag and src/v2 roots only, never src/v1 (documented at dag/test/claim/trait_bound_witness.dag: a prior revision that imported a v1.compiler module failed CI unresolved-import), so no claim can call node_type_shape and assert on its output; (2) the compile instruments that DO execute (compile_dag_diagnostic_census, multi_module_compile_fixture) return rows with NO rendered-text field at all: CompileDiagnosticCensusRow carries diagnostic_class / subject_name / blocking / count, and the fixture's ResolvedRustFnSignature carries owner_module / declaration_name / ordered_parameter_names -- neither has any expected/got/message text, so the collapse is invisible to them (same class, same subject) and the distinct-declaration pair is exactly the shape they cannot separate; (3) the Rust unit-test lane (src/v1/tests) is off the required path (gunbc.rung_drop rust_unit_tests_off_the_merge_path), so a Rust-level string assertion would be green-by-vacancy in the same sense that got the #14485 mint-boundary guard deleted -- its only failing arm was an unauthorable red the review refused to accept as coverage. So a code-only change here moves nothing any executed lane can observe: there is no RED that can be authored today, which is the definition of the thing §4b says must not be shipped as a check.",
    "WHAT IS AND IS NOT CLAIMED HERE (do not read this row as the fix landing). CLAIMED: the defect is real, source-grounded (the applied-head arm of node_type_shape reads node.name and ignores node.declaration), and the reason the fix is held is the missing discriminator, not a judgment that the print is fine. NOT CLAIMED: that the fix is done, that it is tested, or that any lane now catches the collapse. The applied-head-arm change lands LATER and ONLY WITH its discriminator -- as its own change, by whoever builds the instrument below -- so the code and the check that proves it arrive together, which is the §4b rule the held change would otherwise violate.",
    "NEXT-RUNG CARRIER -- THE MISSING INSTRUMENT (this is what the row is waiting on; route set by Charter 4, so the row and the plan agree). The carrier is NOT a new host render intrinsic (Charter 4 ruled one is not to be built). It is a typed rendered-text field ON THE CENSUS ROW THAT ALREADY EXECUTES: CompileDiagnosticCensusRow gains Optional rendered expected/got fields -- Present only where a diagnostic class actually carries such a pair, Absent elsewhere -- plus the head's declaration identity where it is cheap to attach. That field is what turns the collapse of distinct-declaration applications into something a running check can see: today CompileDiagnosticCensusRow's only string fields are diagnostic_class / subject_name (with blocking / count), so two applications of same-named distinct declared carriers produce identical rows (point (2) above) and the collapse is invisible. The retirement route (how this row is deleted): once Charter 4's census-row typed expected/got fields land (queued to bright-swift-151), a dag/ claim compiles the two applications and asserts the census rows' rendered expected/got text DIFFERS when the two heads resolve to distinct declarations (RED before the renderer fix, GREEN after), with a same-declaration control asserting the text still MATCHES (the control that stops the fix from just printing something always-different). When that claim exists and runs on an executing lane, it and the applied-head-arm change land together and this row is deleted in the same change. This row does not schedule or scope Charter 4's instrument work -- it names the field it is waiting on and points at the owner.",
    "LEAVE ALONE WHILE FIXING (the ruling's constraint, recorded so the future fix does not widen). The applied-head arm is one branch; node_type_shape's LEAF arm feeds a consumer in v1.compiler.infer that compares its output to the literal `Primitive(String)`, and there are claims in the claim lane asserting on the Coproduct/Product connective print. Those observe the leaf and connective arms, NOT the applied-head arm, so the applied-head change must not perturb them: the fix adds declaration identity to the `Node(name<args>)` branch only, keeping the leaf arm's `Primitive(...)` text and the connective prints byte-identical. A future author who changes the leaf or connective arms owes those consumers their own measurement, not this row's silence.",
  ],
  evidence: [],
}
